{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Adapting Ethobot Prompts","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting adapting ethobot prompts to Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"07","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Adapting Ethobot Prompts\n\n![Adapting Ethobot Prompts textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-07-adapting-ethobot-prompts.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA default AI prompt is written for an imaginary average class that does not exist in any real room. \"Conduct a Socratic dialogue probing the ethics of surveillance\" assumes students who already know what Socratic means, who can engage an abstraction, and who feel a personal stake in a policy phrased in the third person. Drop that prompt into a 9th-grade class of English learners and you get one-word answers, not because the students lack ideas but because the prompt blocked access to them. Adapting the prompt is how a teacher closes the gap between a tool's defaults and the actual learners in front of her, and doing it well is a precise instructional skill, not a matter of \"simplifying.\"\n\nThe danger that runs alongside adaptation is steering. The easiest way to get richer-looking output is to bake your own opinion into the prompt, at which point the bot stops drawing out student reasoning and starts echoing yours. A defensible adaptation lowers the barrier to participation without tilting the conclusion, and the way you prove you did that is by annotating a before-and-after transcript: the same students, the same topic, with evidence that the change produced their reasoning rather than your answer in their mouths.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Diagnose why a default prompt failed: jargon, abstraction, or missing personal stakes, each with a different fix.\n- Replace opaque framing with a concrete scenario that gives every student an entry point.\n- Constrain the bot's move (one probing follow-up) and add a language scaffold without scaffolding the verdict.\n- Show the effect with a before-and-after transcript, and name the change you rejected because it would steer the conclusion.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher runs the default Ethobot prompt, \"Conduct a Socratic dialogue probing the ethics of surveillance in schools,\" with a 9th-grade class where most students are English learners. The transcript is a wall of one-word answers. It would be easy to read that as disengagement or low ability. Reading it as a prompt failure points to the actual problem: the words \"Socratic\" and \"probing\" are opaque, and \"surveillance in schools\" is an abstraction with no personal hook. The fix is not to lower expectations but to rewrite the prompt so the same students can reach the same ideas, then to prove with a re-run transcript that the rewrite, not luck, made the difference.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAdapting a prompt is targeted redesign, and the target depends on why the default failed. There are three common failure modes, each with its own remedy:\n\n| Failure mode | Symptom | Remedy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Jargon | Students stall on the prompt's own words | Replace academic terms with plain ones |\n| Abstraction | Correct but flat, impersonal answers | Anchor the question in a concrete scenario |\n| No stakes | Polite participation, no investment | Make the prompt name a person who is affected |\n\nTwo design moves do most of the work. The first is to swap abstraction for a concrete scenario: not \"the ethics of surveillance\" but \"your school is thinking about cameras in the hallway, who would feel safer and who would feel watched.\" The second is to constrain the bot's behavior, not the student's answer: instruct the bot to ask one follow-up that makes the student name a person affected, then stop, so it deepens reasoning without flooding or leading.\n\nThe line you must not cross is scaffolding the verdict. Adding a language support (\"if the student gives a one-word answer, ask for an example from their own school day\") helps students reason; adding your opinion (\"guide the student to see that surveillance harms trust\") tells them what to conclude. The first is adaptation; the second is steering, and a re-run transcript is how you check which one you did. This is not just a pedagogical worry: LLMs exhibit documented sycophancy, tailoring answers to the user's stated view even when it is not warranted (Wei et al., 2023; Ranaldi & Pucci, 2023), so a prompt that states the teacher's opinion reliably manufactures agreement.\n\nThe course anchors apply sharply. **Critical edtech studies** is the lens that flags the steering risk: a prompt engineered toward a \"correct\" ethical answer trains compliance, not reasoning. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks whether your adaptation still serves the content goal (ethical reasoning) rather than just producing longer transcripts; current AI-TPACK work extends the same purpose-fit test explicitly to AI-tool integration (Ning et al., 2024). **Teacher professional judgment** is what decides which failure mode you are seeing and what to change, since the bot cannot diagnose its own prompt.\n\n## Worked Example: Adapting a Bot Prompt for a Specific Class\n\nThe default Ethobot prompt may not fit every class. Here a teacher adapts a prompt for a 9th-grade class where most students are English learners, then annotates the effect. Work it as numbered before/after steps.\n\nDefault Ethobot prompt: \"Conduct a Socratic dialogue probing the ethics of surveillance in schools.\"\n\nWhat went wrong in the first run:\n\n1. Students gave one-word answers; the prompt's vocabulary (Socratic, probing) was opaque.\n2. The abstract framing (\"surveillance in schools\") drew no personal stakes.\n\nStep-by-step prompt adaptation:\n\n- Step 1. Replace jargon with a concrete scenario. New opener: \"Your school is thinking about cameras in the hallway. Who would feel safer, and who would feel watched?\"\n- Step 2. Constrain the move, not the answer. Add: \"Ask one follow-up that makes the student name a person affected, then stop.\"\n- Step 3. Add a scaffold for language, not for the verdict. \"If the student gives a one-word answer, ask: can you give an example from your own school day?\"\n- Step 4. Annotate the change in a re-run transcript. Mark where richer reasoning appeared compared to the default run.\n\nBefore (weak adaptation note): \"I simplified the prompt and it worked better.\"\n\nAfter (strong): \"Adapted the prompt by swapping 'Socratic dialogue probing surveillance' for a concrete hallway-camera scenario and adding a one-follow-up scaffold for one-word answers. Evidence it helped: in the re-run, Turn 3 moved from 'cameras bad' to 'my little brother would feel watched but the front office would feel safer.' The change I rejected was adding the teacher's own opinion to the prompt, because that would steer the verdict.\"\n\nThe strong note states the exact prompt edit, shows transcript evidence of the effect, and records a rejected change, which a colleague can audit.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnose, adapt, and prove the prompt fix\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The default prompt was \\\"Conduct a Socratic dialogue probing the ethics of surveillance in schools,\\\" and a 9th-grade EL class gave one-word answers. Before changing anything, name the failure mode for the words Socratic and probing.\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"No stakes: students do not care\",\"Jargon: students stall on the prompt’s own words\",\"Abstraction: correct but flat answers\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Jargon. The opaque academic terms block access before reasoning can begin. The remedy is to replace academic terms with plain ones.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The phrase \\\"surveillance in schools\\\" drew no personal hook. Which failure mode is that, and what is its remedy?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Abstraction; anchor the question in a concrete scenario\",\"Jargon; use shorter words\",\"No failure; the topic is fine\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Abstraction. Anchor it concretely: \\\"Your school is thinking about cameras in the hallway. Who would feel safer, and who would feel watched?\\\"\"},{\"prompt\":\"You add a bot instruction. Which one adapts without steering the verdict?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"\\\"Guide the student to see that surveillance harms trust\\\"\",\"\\\"Ask one follow-up that makes the student name a person affected, then stop\\\"\",\"\\\"Tell the student the right answer if they hesitate\\\"\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Constrain the bot’s move, not the answer. Naming a person affected deepens reasoning; supplying the conclusion is steering, the line you must not cross.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally, prove it with a re-run transcript. If Turn 3 moves from \\\"cameras bad\\\" to \\\"my little brother would feel watched but the front office would feel safer,\\\" the warrant is the student’s own words, and you record the change you rejected (adding your opinion) because it would steer the verdict.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you keep an adapted prompt, run three checks:\n\n1. **Diagnosis:** Did you name the failure mode (jargon, abstraction, stakes) before changing the prompt?\n2. **Open, not steered:** Does the change lower the barrier to participation without telling students what to conclude?\n3. **Evidence:** Does a before-and-after transcript show the change, in a student's own words, produced the richer reasoning?\n\nIf your evidence is \"it felt better,\" you have a hunch; the re-run transcript is what turns it into a defensible adaptation.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher adds to the prompt: 'Guide the student to conclude that hallway cameras violate trust.' The re-run transcript is longer and every student agrees cameras are bad. Why is this a warning sign, not a success?\" explanation=\"Richer output that all converges on the teacher''s opinion is manufactured agreement: the prompt told students what to conclude, so the bot is echoing the teacher rather than drawing out student reasoning. That is steering, the line a defensible adaptation must not cross.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is a success; longer and agreed-upon means it worked\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The agreement was steered by the prompt, not produced by student reasoning\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The prompt is still too abstract\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The bot asked too many follow-ups\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe most tempting shortcut to a rich-looking transcript is to bake your opinion in. Walk the choice that separates adaptation from steering.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Adapt without steering\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your hallway-camera scenario got better answers, but a few students still freeze on one-word replies. You want richer reasoning. What do you add to the prompt?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"Lead the student to see that cameras protect students, so monitoring is good\\\"\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"This bakes your conclusion into the prompt. The bot will now echo your verdict, not draw out the student’s reasoning.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"If the student gives a one-word answer, ask for an example from their own school day\\\"\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This is a language scaffold, not a verdict scaffold: it lowers the barrier to participation without telling students what to conclude.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"Have the bot list the three best arguments against cameras\\\"\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Supplying the arguments does the reasoning for the student and quietly tilts the dialogue.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what is the difference between helping a student reason and telling them what to conclude?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A scaffold lowers the barrier; an opinion supplies the answer\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Adapt the access, not the verdict.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You re-run with the scaffold. Turn 3 moves from \\\"cameras bad\\\" to \\\"my little brother would feel watched but the front office would feel safer.\\\" How do you prove the adaptation, not luck, caused this?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Quote the before-and-after lines from the same students on the same topic\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"This is the evidence standard: a quoted before-and-after in the student’s own words.\"},{\"label\":\"Note that the transcript felt more engaged overall\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"A feeling is not evidence. You need the quoted before-and-after.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor: who should generate the arguments?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The student; the bot only asks one follow-up\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Constrain the bot’s move, not the student’s reasoning.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"You record the change you rejected. Which one, and why?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adding your own opinion to the prompt, because it would steer the verdict\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Recording the rejected steering change is what makes the adaptation auditable.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Replace the impression with quoted evidence.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Quote the Turn 3 before-and-after\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Now the claim is defensible.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You adapted with a language scaffold, proved the effect with quoted lines, and rejected the steering change.\",\"feedback\":\"Defensible adaptation lowers the barrier to participation without tilting the conclusion, and a re-run transcript is how you prove which one you did.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading equates adaptation with simplification, as if shorter words alone fix a flat dialogue; often the real failure is missing stakes, and a simpler abstraction is still an abstraction. The second, more serious error is treating a more agreeable transcript as proof of success when the agreement was manufactured by a prompt that told students what to say. Richer output that all converges on your opinion is a warning sign, not a win.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the diagnosis to a different misfire. A teacher's default prompt, \"Debate the trolley problem and justify your utilitarian reasoning,\" produces blank stares in a 7th-grade class. Diagnose: the failure is jargon (\"utilitarian\") plus an abstraction with no personal hook. Adapt: \"Your bus can only pick up one of two groups before the bell, the soccer team or the band, and both will be late if you choose wrong. Who do you pick, and what makes it fair?\" Constrain the bot to one follow-up that asks the student to name who loses. Add a scaffold for one-word answers. Then re-run and annotate: if Turn 4 moves from \"the soccer team\" to \"the band, because they have a concert tonight and the team just has practice, so the band loses more,\" the adaptation worked, and the warrant is the student's own comparative reasoning, not a verdict you supplied. The same diagnose-adapt-prove cycle handles any prompt that lands flat.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: the prompt change on the left, the transcript evidence of its effect on the right, quoted from a student. A strong reader of an adaptation can name the failure mode it addressed and point to the line in the re-run that proves the fix, and can also name the tempting change they rejected to avoid steering. If your note claims improvement without a quoted before-and-after, you have not yet shown the adaptation did anything.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong adaptation note states the diagnosed failure mode, the exact prompt edit, transcript evidence of the effect in a student's words, and the steering change you rejected and why. Polish is the last layer. If the note lacks before-and-after evidence, mark the gap and ask BigAL to compare your adapted prompt against one that subtly steers, so you can see the line you are protecting.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I replaced the abstract surveillance framing with a hallway-camera scenario to fix a missing-stakes failure; the re-run shows a student moving from 'cameras bad' to naming who feels watched and who feels safer, and I rejected adding my own opinion to the prompt because it would have produced agreement instead of reasoning.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain the difference between a language scaffold ('if the answer is one word, ask for an example from your school day') and a verdict scaffold ('guide the student to see surveillance harms trust'), and why only the first counts as adaptation.\" answer=\"A language scaffold lowers the barrier to participation: it gives a stuck student a way back into reasoning without telling them where to land, so whatever conclusion they reach is still their own. A verdict scaffold supplies the conclusion in advance, so the bot stops drawing out the student''s reasoning and starts producing agreement with the teacher''s view. Only the first is adaptation, because adaptation closes the gap between the tool''s defaults and the actual learners without changing what the activity is for, which is student ethical reasoning. The second is steering: it trains compliance, and a re-run transcript that all converges on the teacher''s opinion is the tell.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the adaptation skill to a class on the opposite end: gifted seniors who find the concrete hallway scenario too easy and disengage from its simplicity. Here the failure mode flips from \"no access\" to \"no challenge,\" and the fix is to add complexity (a budget constraint, a competing legal requirement) rather than to simplify. Working this version tests whether you adapt by diagnosing the specific learners in front of you, or whether \"adapt\" has quietly come to mean only \"make it simpler.\"\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-07-adapting-ethobot-prompts-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed for CAT 531 04.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how diagnosing a prompt failure and proving the fix with a transcript is evidence-based revision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. *Learning, Media and Technology, 45*(3). [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Williamson, B., et al. (2023). Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. *Learning, Media and Technology, 48*(1). https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Wei, J., et al. (2023). Simple synthetic data reduces sycophancy in large language models. https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2308.03958\n- Ranaldi, L., & Pucci, G. (2023). When large language models contradict humans? Large language models' sycophantic behaviour. https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2311.09410\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","stem":"The default prompt 'Conduct a Socratic dialogue probing the ethics of surveillance' produces one-word answers from a class of English learners. What is the most likely teaching problem?","options":["The students are not interested in ethics.","The prompt's abstract framing and jargon do not fit this class, so it needs adaptation rather than blame.","The Ethobot model is broken and should be replaced.","The topic of surveillance is inappropriate for ninth grade."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A default prompt is not universal; thin responses signal a need to adapt framing and vocabulary to the class.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_default_prompt_universal","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","stem":"A teacher adapts the prompt by adding 'Tell students that hallway cameras are a violation of privacy, then discuss.' Why is this adaptation poor?","options":["It is longer than the original prompt.","It steers students to a predetermined verdict instead of scaffolding how they reason.","It mentions hallways, which are not academic.","It uses the word violation, which is too strong."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Adapting should improve the reasoning move, not bake the conclusion into the prompt.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_steering_the_verdict","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","stem":"Which prompt scaffold supports English learners without steering the verdict?","options":["If the student disagrees with cameras, tell them they are correct.","If the student gives a one-word answer, ask for an example from their own school day.","Tell the student the ethical conclusion the class should reach.","Stop the dialogue after the first short answer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A language scaffold elicits more reasoning without dictating the conclusion.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_steering_the_verdict","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_adapting_ethobot_prompts_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A teacher reports 'I simplified the prompt and it worked better.' What single addition would make this a defensible evidence claim?","options":["The total number of words removed from the prompt.","A comparison of a turn from the default run and the adapted run showing where reasoning became richer.","The date the prompt was changed.","A note that students seemed happier."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transcript evidence comparing the two runs makes the improvement claim auditable.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_7_change_without_evidence","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_adapting_ethobot_prompts"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"fefe030cf1c588910ddce3345dfe636dfef8202947385e71ef980cf2bf805e07","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}